<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07782v1/ppl_vs_pruning_bar_wanda.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/343;" width="548" height="343"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>WANDA-sp score-based pruning with under different update methods.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07782v1/ppl_vs_pruning_bar_prop.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/343;" width="548" height="343"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Proposed score-based pruning under different update methods.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Perplexity versus pruning ratio for different pruning scores and update methods.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2 Perplexity ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ RCPU: Rotation-Constrained Error Compensation for Structured Pruning of a Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes perplexity on WikiText-2 across pruning ratios.
As we can see from <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.2 Perplexity ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ RCPU: Rotation-Constrained Error Compensation for Structured Pruning of a Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, RCPU consistently improves upon WANDA-sp and performs on par with or better than FLAP.
These results show that rotation-constrained updates can be competitive with bias-based correction, despite offering higher degrees of freedom.
While the advantage is not uniform at all pruning levels, the geometry-preserving nature of rotational transformations helps prevent the distortions that often arise from unconstrained updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also compare the scaled variant (Rot.+Scale) to plain rotation.
The results show only marginal differences, indicating that introducing a global rescaling does not significantly alter perplexity.
We believe this is because the rotation already aligns the retained subspace with the dominant output directions, effectively discarding less informative components.
As a result, the overall output norm is well preserved, and an additional scaling factor brings little further benefit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.2 Perplexity ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ RCPU: Rotation-Constrained Error Compensation for Structured Pruning of a Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> focuses on the effect of different error-compensation methods (LS or Rot.).
Compared to no compensation, rotation-constrained updates consistently suppress the growth of perplexity, and the benefit becomes more pronounced at higher pruning ratios.
In contrast, least-squares fitting provides little to no improvement, and in many cases even worsens perplexity, reflecting its tendency to overfit under limited calibration.
Another notable trend is that within both WANDA-sp-based and variance-aware scores, rotation compensation is consistently the most effective.
Considering that the only difference between the two scores is the inclusion of an input-variance factor, the results suggest that variance-aware scoring further strengthens orientation recovery by retaining columns that align with dominant output directions.
Taken together, these findings indicate that beyond the choice of compensation method, the proposed variance-aware score itself offers a stronger foundation for structured pruning, yielding lower perplexity across pruning ratios compared to WANDA-sp-based scoring.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Benchmark</h3>

